WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.960
Part 3. You will hear two food science students called Marie and Colin discussing their final

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:16.879
year projects. First, you have some time to look at questions 21 to 24.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:45.639
Now, listen carefully and answer questions 21 to 24.

00:00:45.639 --> 00:00:50.919
I haven't seen you for a bit, Marie. No, I've been busy with my project.

00:00:50.919 --> 00:00:56.279
You're making a vegan alternative to eggs, aren't you? Something that doesn't use

00:00:56.279 --> 00:01:01.840
animal products. Yes, I'm using chickpeas. I had two main

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:08.239
aims when I first started looking for an alternative to eggs. But actually, I've found chickpeas

00:01:08.239 --> 00:01:11.160
have got more advantages. Right.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:18.680
But how about your project on reusing waste food? You were looking at bread, weren't

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:22.000
you? Yes. It's been hard work, but I've enjoyed

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:29.239
it. The basic process was quite straightforward – breaking the stale bread down to a paste

00:01:29.239 --> 00:01:34.279
then reforming it. But you were using 3D printing, weren't you,

00:01:34.279 --> 00:01:39.040
to make the paste into biscuits? Yes, I'd used that before, but in this

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:45.279
project I had time to play around with different patterns for the biscuits and finding how

00:01:45.279 --> 00:01:51.199
I could add fruit and vegetables to make them a more appetising colour. And I was really

00:01:51.199 --> 00:01:56.739
pleased with what I managed to produce. It must have been a great feeling to make

00:01:56.739 --> 00:02:02.639
something appetising out of bits of old bread that would have been thrown away otherwise.

00:02:02.639 --> 00:02:08.160
It was. And I'm hoping that some of the restaurants in town will be interested in

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:11.880
the biscuits. I'm going to send them some samples.

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:17.960
I came across something on the internet yesterday that might interest you. It was a company

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:24.919
that's developed touch-sensitive sensors for food labels. It's a special sort of

00:02:24.919 --> 00:02:31.679
label on the food package. When the label's smooth, the food is fresh, and then when you

00:02:31.679 --> 00:02:38.559
can feel bumps on the label, that means the food's gone bad. It started off as a project

00:02:38.559 --> 00:02:44.320
to help visually impaired people know whether food was fit to eat or not.

00:02:45.039 --> 00:02:47.039
So just solid food?

00:02:47.039 --> 00:02:54.240
No, things like milk and juice as well. But actually I thought it might be really good

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:57.520
for drug storage in hospitals and pharmacies.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:04.639
Right. And coming back to food, maybe it'd be possible to use it for other things besides

00:03:04.639 --> 00:03:09.839
freshness, like how many kilograms a joint of meat is, for example.

00:03:09.839 --> 00:03:12.720
Yes, there's all sorts of possibilities.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:23.320
Before you hear the rest of the discussion, you have some time to look at questions 25 to 30.

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:58.320
Now listen and answer questions 25 to 30.

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:07.320
I was reading an article about food trends predicting how eating habits might change

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:09.320
in the next few years.

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:16.320
Things like more focus on local products. That seems so obvious, but the shops are still

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:18.320
full of imported foods.

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:21.320
Yes, they need to be more proactive to address that.

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:25.320
And somehow motivate consumers to change, yes.

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:31.320
One thing everyone's aware of is the need for a reduction in unnecessary packaging.

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:37.320
But just about everything you buy in supermarkets is still covered in plastic.

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:40.320
The government needs to do something about it.

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:43.320
Absolutely. It's got to change.

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:49.320
Do you think there'll be more interest in gluten and lactose-free food?

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:55.320
For people with allergies or food intolerances? I don't know.

00:04:55.320 --> 00:05:00.320
Lots of people I know have been buying that type of food for years now.

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:03.320
Yes, even if they haven't been diagnosed with an allergy.

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:05.320
That's right.

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:12.320
One thing I've noticed is the number of branded products related to celebrity chefs.

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:19.320
People watch them cooking on TV and then buy things like spice mixes or frozen foods

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:21.320
with the chef's name on.

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:25.320
I bought something like that once, but I won't again.

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:31.320
Yeah, I bought a ready-made spice mix for chicken which was supposed to be used by a

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:36.320
chef I'd seen on television and it didn't actually taste of anything.

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:42.320
Hmm. Did the article mention ghost kitchens used to produce takeaway food?

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:44.320
No. What are they?

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:50.320
Well, they might have the name of a restaurant, but actually they're a cooking facility

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:54.320
just for delivery meals. The public don't ever go there.

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:58.320
But people aren't aware of that. It's all kept very quiet.

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:03.320
So people don't realise the food's not actually from the restaurant?

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:05.320
Right.

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:10.320
Did you know more and more people are using all sorts of different mushrooms now

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:15.320
to treat different health concerns, things like heart problems?

00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:19.320
Hmm. They might be taking a big risk there.

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:24.320
Yes, it's hard to know which varieties are safe to eat.

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:26.320
Anyway, maybe now we should...

00:06:29.320 --> 00:06:32.320
That is the end of part three.

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:37.320
You now have thirty seconds to check your answers to part three.

00:06:58.320 --> 00:06:59.320
.

